<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
Fleet dead-man watcher.

(No double hyphen may appear anywhere in this comment. XML forbids it inside a
comment; launchd tolerates it and strict parsers do not, and two live plists in
this tree already carry that bug. Long flags are therefore named in prose, and
cabinet/scripts/fleet-deadman-install.py prints the exact command lines.)

WHY THE LABEL IS NOT com.cabinet.something. READ THIS BEFORE RENAMING IT.
The whole fleet is torn down, and was torn down on 2026-07-25, by unloading
every com.cabinet.* label at once. A watcher inside that label space is removed
by the same command that removes what it watches, which is the exact failure
this job exists to survive. The prefix is deliberately different, and
cabinet/scripts/deploy-mac.sh and generate-plists.py, which render and install
the fleet, do not know this file exists. That is the point, not an oversight.

AND BE HONEST ABOUT WHAT THAT BUYS. macOS offers no privilege-free domain
isolation: gui/<uid> is the only domain a non-root user can bootstrap into
(bootstrapping user/<uid> or system returns "Bootstrap failed: 5" without root,
measured 2026-07-31), so this job shares a domain with the fleet and survives
only because the teardown names a prefix. A broader teardown, a logout, or a
reboot without login takes it too.

So this is the SECOND leg, never the only one. The leg that actually survives
this machine is the off-machine dead-man: this job pings an external watcher
ONLY when it has measured the fleet ALIVE, so the external watcher alarms on
absence whether the fleet died, this job died, or the box died. Configure it in
instance/config/liveness.yml under the fleet_alive slug, and confirm both legs:

    python3.12 cabinet/scripts/fleet-deadman.py   (with the status flag)

INSTALL, a human act. Nothing in the repo bootstraps this for you:

    python3.12 cabinet/scripts/fleet-deadman-install.py   (with the install flag)

then run the launchctl bootstrap line it prints. It never runs it for you.

DISABLE:

    launchctl bootout gui/$(id -u)/com.cabinet-liveness.fleet-deadman
    rm ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.cabinet-liveness.fleet-deadman.plist

rm alone does NOT stop a loaded job (measured 2026-07-31). Bootout first.

EXIT STATUS IS THE VERDICT: 0 ALIVE, 1 DEAD, 2 UNKNOWN. A standing 1 in
launchctl list is a true report about the fleet, not a broken job.

That claim is only safe because the watcher now REFUSES to spend exit 1 on its
own failure (corrected 2026-07-31 — it shipped exiting 1 out of an uncaught
NameError, so a crashed watcher was documented as a truthful death report).
Anything the watcher cannot do — resolve its store, complete a pass, survive an
unexpected error — is UNKNOWN and exits 2. So: 1 means the fleet, 2 means the
watcher or the question. Neither pings, so the external check alarms either way;
the distinction is for whoever reads launchctl.

NO SECRETS HERE. The external ping URL and slug live in
instance/config/liveness.yml; this plist carries paths and PATH only.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>com.cabinet-liveness.fleet-deadman</string>

  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/usr/bin/env</string>
    <string>python3.12</string>
    <string>${REPO_ROOT}/cabinet/scripts/fleet-deadman.py</string>
  </array>

  <key>WorkingDirectory</key>
  <string>${REPO_ROOT}</string>

  <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
  <dict>
    <key>HOME</key><string>${HOME}</string>
    <key>USER</key><string>${USER}</string>
    <key>PYTHONPATH</key><string>${REPO_ROOT}</string>
    <key>CABINET_ENV</key><string>runtime</string>
    <key>PATH</key><string>/opt/homebrew/bin:/opt/homebrew/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin</string>
    <key>LANG</key><string>en_US.UTF-8</string>
  </dict>

  <key>StartInterval</key>
  <integer>900</integer>

  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <true/>

  <key>StandardOutPath</key>
  <string>${HOME}/.cabinet/logs/fleet-deadman.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
  <string>${HOME}/.cabinet/logs/fleet-deadman.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
